e 4B model, diagnostic analysis of degeneracy and compression, and the supervised verifier baseline. I need to stay consistent with the resolved numbers from the claim audit throughout.\n\nWriting the LaTeX structure... Looking at the data more carefully, the 0.82 AUROC must come from Qwen on CoNLL since the experiment_data.json only shows LLaMA results (LP_AUROC 0.752, EM_AUROC 0.701). Now I'm checking the SciERC evidence from paper_framing to see what metrics are actually available there—NER shows EM AUROC at 0.749 and RE results are partially listed. Now I'm looking at the SciERC results for both NER and relation extraction tasks, comparing greedy decoding against oracle performance. I need to structure the paper to cover each experiment from the data file systematically, so I'll organize sections around the different model-dataset combinations and make sure the audit findings are integrated as validation rather than standalone content. I'm settling on a title that captures the core finding, then planning out the key figures I need—a schematic overview, a comparison of selection methods against the signal strength, and a visualization showing how capability and degradation correlate. I'll use pgfplots to keep things simple and reproducible rather than getting bogged down in complex graphics.\n\nSince this is a tight paper, I'm leaning toward tables for the data-heavy sections. For the claim audit, I'll describe the systematic review process in the main text and relegate the detailed results table to the appendix—that way it shows we did the work without cluttering the narrative. I also need to add a positioning paragraph in Related Work comparing against recent work like CoCoA and Vashurin 2025.\n\nSetting up the LaTeX document now...\n\nWriting the paper structure... I'm reconsidering the structure—the claim audit is really about the paper's internal consistency, so I'll move those numbers into an appendix table rather than making it a full section. Now I'm pulling together the key experimental results to verify: the main SciERC numbers from Qwen3-8B across NER and RE tasks, checking the Spearman correlations, AUROC scores, and calibration metrics that will anchor the paper's findings. Looking at the bootstrap comparison between LP and greedy on SciERC, the confidence interval spans zero with no significant difference. Now I'm examining results across different model scales—LLaMA on CoNLL shows greedy and LP performing nearly identically around 0.921, while the 4B ablation reveals similar patterns with SJ and FK having comparable correlation scores.\n\nFor SciERC NER at 4B scale, greedy maintains an edge over SJ and LP, though the gaps narrow considerably. The LoRA rank=8 experiments on SciERC show that lower rank reduces degeneracy substantially compared to rank=32, and while oracle headroom decreases slightly, selection methods like LP and SJ still underperform greedy by less than a percentage point.\n\nLooking at the quality estimation metrics, I'm seeing that VC and SJ correlations are both moderate. In the free-form ablation, the constrained and free-form LP standards are nearly equivalent, with tied fractions dropping slightly and parse rates remaining near perfect. The supervised verifier results are interesting—it closes the gap to oracle by 8.3% on NER and 4.4% on RE, outperforming both greedy and LP baselines. Cross-validation AUROC is solid for both tasks, and the feature importance ranking shows exact match dominates, followed by voting confidence, then SJ, LP, and FK.\n\nI'm settling on 0.82 as the AUROC ceiling based on the CoNLL EM result. For the signal families, I'm consolidating to three: structural signals (SJ, FK, EM), voting confidence, and probabilistic LP. I'll include the claim audit as an appendix to build reviewer trust, and I'm ready to start writing the paper in LaTeX. I need to be careful with how I frame the contributions—the evaluation should be positioned as systematically confirming and extending existing uncertainty quantification principles to structured information extraction, not as introducing something entirely novel. The diagnostic analysis needs hedging too; I'm identifying contributing factors rather than claiming to have mapped out the complete causal picture. I should drop \"capability-degeneracy tradeoff\" as a named contribution and keep it as an observed pattern in the discussion instead. I also need to add a paragraph directly comparing against CoCoA and Vashurin 2025 to address the incrementality concern upfront. Since the verifier shows weak performance with only an 8.3% gap closure, I'll frame that as preliminary evidence that supervised approaches are necessary and expand on it in future work. Now I'm ready to write out the full LaTeX.\n\nSetting up the paper structure with pgfplots for visualizations—I'll create bar charts comparing selection F1 across different signals against greedy and oracle baselines for SciERC tasks, plus grouped comparisons across models. For reliability and compilation, I'm keeping the visualization approach minimal and relying more on tables, using a standard LaTeX setup that will definitely compile.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":198,"cache_read_input_tokens":37904,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":198},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6243f3b-91fc-4875-8c31-fd34d0e76126","uuid":"3a321a1b-a10c-4462-8548-e267b7d950a0"}
